      "title": "Workplace Health, Safety & OSHA Compliance: Ergonomics, Incident Logging & Workers' Comp",
      "summary": "Engineer workplace safety and statutory health governance: the Occupational Safety and Health Act (OSHA), Form 300/300A/301 logging, ergonomics, workplace violence prevention, and Workers' Comp administration.",
      "readingTimeMinutes": 39,
      "videoUrl": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F3_6eO8bQ2w",
      "body": "<h2>1. The General Duty Clause & Workplace Safety Mandate</h2>\n<p>Under Section 5(a)(1) of the Occupational Safety and Health Act of 1970 (known as the <strong>General Duty Clause</strong>), every employer has an immutable legal obligation to furnish to each of its employees a workplace that is free from recognized hazards that are causing or are likely to cause death or serious physical harm. Workplace safety is not limited to heavy manufacturing or construction; modern tech and corporate environments face serious OSHA liabilities concerning repetitive strain injuries, ergonomic hazards, fire safety, indoor air quality, and workplace violence.</p>\n<h2>2. OSHA Recordkeeping & Injury Logging (Forms 300, 301, 300A)</h2>\n<p>Employers with more than 10 employees (unless in low-hazard exempt retail/office classifications, though incident logging remains standard) must record work-related injuries and illnesses:</p>\n<table class=\"comparison-table\">\n  <thead><tr><th>OSHA Form</th><th>Full Name</th><th>Purpose & Timing</th><th>Posting / Submission Rules</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td><strong>Form 300</strong></td><td>Log of Work-Related Injuries and Illnesses</td><td>Continuous chronological record of recordable injuries, days away, job restrictions.</td><td>Maintained on ongoing basis; kept for 5 years.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Form 301</strong></td><td>Injury and Illness Incident Report</td><td>Detailed narrative of each individual incident (how it happened, medical treatment).</td><td>Completed within 7 calendar days of notice.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Form 300A</strong></td><td>Summary of Work-Related Injuries and Illnesses</td><td>Annual executive summary of total cases, days lost, and employee hours worked.</td><td>Must be certified by an executive and posted visibly in workplace from <strong>Feb 1 to April 30</strong> each year.</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>What Constitutes a 'Recordable' Injury?</h3>\n<p>An injury or illness is OSHA recordable if it is work-related AND results in: death, loss of consciousness, days away from work, restricted work or transfer to another job, or medical treatment beyond first aid.</p>\n<h2>3. Mandatory Immediate OSHA Reporting Timelines</h2>\n<pre><code>- Work-related FATALITY: Must report to OSHA within 8 HOURS.\n- In-patient hospitalization, amputation, or eye loss: Must report within 24 HOURS.</code></pre>\n<h2>4. Workers' Compensation Insurance Lifecycle</h2>\n<p>Workers' Compensation is a statutory no-fault insurance system that provides medical coverage and lost wage replacement to employees injured in the course of employment, while granting employers immunity from civil tort lawsuits (the 'Exclusive Remedy' rule). HR leads the claims process: ensuring immediate medical triage, filing First Report of Injury (FROI) within statutory state windows (typically 24-72 hours), and managing Return-to-Work (RTW) light-duty programs.</p>\n<h2>5. Ergonomics and Workplace Violence Prevention</h2>\n<p>With remote and desk-based knowledge workers, repetitive strain injuries (carpal tunnel, lumbar spine disorders) represent the highest volume of workers' comp claims. HR establishes proactive ergonomic evaluations, sit-stand desk policies, and comprehensive Workplace Violence Prevention Plans (zero-tolerance threat reporting, building access control, active threat drills).</p>"
